Z||000015||Taijiguy||04-20-2001||07:11 AM||||Well, gray smoke can be blue-gray, or black-gray. If it's bluish, it's oil. If it's black, it's fuel. The latter is probably better, since it's gonna be easier to address. Black smoke could be a stuck injector, or the cold start injector may not disable when the car warms, is the motor reaching operating temperatures? Black means you need to troubleshoot the fuel delivery system.<BR>If it's blue, that's bad, and could be alot of things: If it puffs when you first start it, and then stops, that's usually valve stem seals. If it smokes any other time, it's generally rings. <BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000016||MooCow||04-20-2001||11:12 AM||||It is more more black than blue.  I dont even see blus smoke comming out at all.  The only time smoke comes out is when I hit the gas and when I first start it up.  Also the engine never does get to hot to touch on the plenumn.  Usually after I run the car around my block the engine is almost to hot to touch.  Yesterday I ran it around the block twice and still was not hot.  So you guys and gals think it is my injectors??<P>                             Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000017||Taijiguy||04-20-2001||09:18 PM||||Sounds like it's running rich. To just say "the injectors" is pretty ambiguous. I'm not super heavy in all the ECM stuff, someone else (Like Ogre or Oreif) may be more qualified to narrow it down for you. I doubt it's the injectors. Check to see if there are any codes.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
